L000342	\title{First Experimental Limit on the Permanent Electric Dipole Moment of the Deuteron}
L000343	
L000344	\include{authors}
L000345	
L000346	\collaboration{JEDI Collaboration}
L000347	\noaffiliation
L000348	
L000349	\begin{abstract}
L000350	    Permanent electric dipole moments (EDMs) provide a sensitive probe of physics beyond the Standard Model and are directly linked to additional sources of CP violation that could explain the matter-antimatter asymmetry of the universe. EDM measurements of charged particles in storage rings rely on detecting a small tilt of the invariant spin axis with respect to the ring plane. In this work, we present the experimental determination of the invariant spin axis of an ensemble of deuterons in the COoler SYnchrotron (COSY), a conventional magnetic storage ring, using a combination of a radio-frequency Wien filter, a superconducting Siberian snake and an electron-cooler solenoid. The measurements reveal tilts of a few milliradians, which are dominated by systematic effects. From the observed tilts, we derive the first experimental limit on the deuteron EDM, $|d^d|<\SI{2.5e-17}{\echarge\cdot cm} \; (\SI{95}{\%} \text{ C.L.})$. This result demonstrates the feasibility of using storage rings to search for EDMs of charged stable hadrons and provides a foundation for future dedicated facilities.
L000351	\end{abstract}
L000352	
L000353	\maketitle
L000354	
L000355	Permanent electric dipole moments (EDMs) of elementary and composed sub-atomic particles violate both parity (P) and time-reversal (T) symmetry~\cite{Khriplovich:1997ga,Pospelov:2005pr}.
L000356	If the combined CPT symmetry -- where C represents charge conjugation -- is conserved, then T violation implies CP violation~\cite{Luders:1957zz,Greenberg:2002uu}.
L000357	So far CP violation has been observed, for example, in the decays of neutral kaons~\cite{PhysRevLett.13.138}, B mesons~\cite{Gershon_2017,Aubert:2001nu} and baryons~\cite{LHCb:2025ray}.
L000358	The Standard Model predicts EDMs so tiny that their detection is beyond the current experimental reach~\cite{Engel:2013lsa,chupp:2017rkp}.
L000359	Consequently, any measurable EDM would be a hint at the presence of physics beyond the Standard Model (or a non-zero QCD $\theta$ term)~\cite{Pospelov:2005pr}.
L000360	Therefore, EDMs are considered to be sensitive probes to search for additional sources of CP violation, which are needed to explain the observed matter-antimatter asymmetry in the universe~\cite{Sakharov:1967dj}.
L000361	This paper discusses the first measurement of the deuteron EDM building on a sequence of milestones previously achieved and published by the JEDI collaboration~\cite{Guidoboni:2016bdn,Eversmann:2015jnk,PhysRevAccelBeams.20.072801,PhysRevResearch.7.023257,PhysRevAccelBeams.28.062801}.
L000362	
L000363	In general, an EDM of a sub-atomic particle can be measured by studying its influence on the particle's spin motion, since the EDM is aligned with its spin direction.
L000364	Quantitatively, the spin dynamics is governed by the Thomas-BMT equation~\cite{Bargmann:1959gz,Nelson:1959zz,Fukuyama:2013ioa}.
L000365	In a purely magnetic storage ring with a vertical magnetic field and $\vec\beta \perp \vec B$, the spin evolution relative to the momentum vector can be described in a co-rotating frame.
L000366	Subtracting the cyclotron rotation removes all kinematic and Dirac ($g=2$) contributions, such that the remaining spin precession is governed solely by the anomalous magnetic moment $G$ and a possible EDM (see, \emph{e.g.},\cite{chupp:2017rkp}).
L000367	Neglecting terms proportional to $\vec\beta\cdot\vec B$, the resulting equation of motion reads:
L000368	\begin{eqnarray}
L000369	\frac{\text{d} \vec{S}}{\text{d}t} = \vec\Omega_S \times \vec{S} &=& (\vec{\Omega}_G + \vec{\Omega}_{\mathrm{EDM}}) \times \vec{S},  \label{eq:tbmt}\\
L000370	 \vec{\Omega}_G &=&  -\frac{q}{m} ~ G \vec B \label{eq:tbmt_mdm},\\
L000371	 \vec{\Omega}_{\mathrm{EDM}} &=& -\frac{q}{m} ~\frac{\eta}{2} \,  \vec{\beta} \times \vec{B}. \label{eq:omedm}
L000372	\end{eqnarray}
L000373	Here, $\vec\Omega_G$ denotes exclusively the anomalous magnetic-moment-induced spin precession
L000374	and $\vec{\Omega}_{\mathrm{EDM}}$ the EDM-induced spin rotation.
L000375	$\vec{S}$ is the spin vector in units of $\hbar$ in the particle rest frame, $t$ the time in the laboratory system, $m$ and $q$ the rest mass and the charge of the particle, $\beta$ the particle velocity in units of $c$ and $\vec{B}$ the magnetic field in the laboratory system.
L000376	The dimensionless quantities $G$ and $\eta$ are related to the magnetic dipole moment (MDM) $\vec \mu$ and the EDM $\vec d$:
L000377	\begin{equation}
L000378	\vec{\mu} =
L000379	g \frac{q \hbar}{2 m} \vec{S} =
L000380	(1+G) \frac{q \hbar}{m} \vec{S}\, \quad \mbox{and} \quad \vec{d} = \eta \frac{q \hbar}{2 m c} \vec{S} \, .
L000381	\label{eq:mdm_and_edm}
L000382	\end{equation}
L000383	With the given assumptions, the anomalous magnetic moment and an EDM cause precessions around the vertical $y$-axis and the radial $x$-axis, respectively (see Fig.~\ref{fig:ISA_rotation}):
L000384	\begin{alignat}{2}
L000385	\vec{\Omega}_G &{}={}& \Omega_G&\;\vec{e}_\mathrm{y},  \\
L000386	\vec{\Omega}_\mathrm{EDM} &{}={}& \Omega_\mathrm{EDM}&\;\vec{e}_\mathrm{x}.
L000387	\end{alignat}
L000388	\begin{figure}
L000389	    \centering
L000390	    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figure_1.pdf}
L000391	    \caption{Influence of the EDM on the invariant spin axis $\vec n$. The gray line marks the beam trajectory. Assuming an idealized storage ring and no EDM, the spin is precessing in the $x$-$z$ plane. A non-zero EDM tilts the spin-precession plane around the $z$-axis by an angle $\xiedm$ indicated by the blue plane. The situation shown here corresponds to a particle with $G<0$, e.g. a deuteron.
L000392	    The spin $\vec s$ precesses in opposite direction to the momentum $\vec p$. For a positive EDM, i.e. $\eta >0$, the angle $\xiedm$ defined in Eq.~(\ref{eq:eta_edm}) is negative.} \label{fig:ISA_rotation}
L000393	\end{figure}
L000394	It is convenient to introduce the invariant spin axis $\vec n$ as a unit vector normal to the tilted precession plane, \ie $\vec\Omega_S = \Omega_S\,\vec{n}$, and its tilt angle $\xiedm$ in the $x$-$y$ plane such that
L000395	\begin{equation}\label{eq:n}
L000396	\vec n=\left(-\sin\xiedm,\cos\xiedm,0 \right).
L000397	\end{equation}
L000398	With $|\eta|\ll 1$ one gets to first order in $\eta$
L000399	\begin{equation}
L000400	\label{eq:eta_edm}
L000401	\xiedm=-\frac{\Omega_{\mathrm{EDM}}}{\Omega_G} = \frac{\eta \beta}{2 G}.
L000402	\end{equation}
L000403	Solving Eq. (\ref{eq:tbmt}) one finds for a spin vector initially pointing in longitudinal direction ($\vec S(0) = (0,0,1)$) to first order in $\eta$
L000404	\begin{equation}\label{eq:St}
L000405	\vec S(t) = \left(\sin(\Omega_G t), \xiedm \sin(\Omega_G t) ,\cos(\Omega_G t)\right),
L000406	\end{equation}
L000407	\ie the EDM causes an oscillation of the vertical polarization component with amplitude $|\xiedm|$. This signature is used in the muon $g-2$ experiment to measure the muon EDM \cite{PhysRevD.80.052008}.
L000408	For deuterons this approach is far less sensitive because the magnitude of the magnetic anomaly $G$ is much larger than that of the muon.
L000409	
L000410	To induce a macroscopic build-up of a vertical polarization an additional radio-frequency (rf) Wien filter \cite{Rathmann:2013rqa,PhysRevSTAB.16.114001} in resonance with the spin precession is applied such that the Lorentz force vanishes \cite{Slim:2016pim} and the electric and magnetic fields only act on the spin vector.
L000411	For a bunched beam, the rf Wien filter applies a spin kick turn by turn and is operated at a spin-resonance frequency corresponding to the $k^\textrm{th}$ harmonic of the revolution frequency.
L000412	The additional spin rotation around the magnetic field axis $\vec{m}$ of the rf Wien filter per turn is
L000413	\begin{eqnarray}
L000414	    \psi(t) &=& \psi_0 \sin(\Omega_\mathrm{WF} t + \phirel) \quad \mbox{with} \label{eq:wfrot}\\
L000415	    \psi_0 &=& \frac{q}{m} \frac{G+1}{\gamma^2\beta c} \int B_\textrm{WF}\,\text{d}l, \\
L000416	    \Omega_\mathrm{WF} &=& | k\,\Omega_\mathrm{rev} + \Omega_S | \,,\quad k\in\mathbb{Z}\,,
L000417	\end{eqnarray}
L000418	where $B_\textrm{WF}$ and $\Omega_\mathrm{WF}$ denote the magnetic field  and the radio frequency of the rf Wien filter, respectively, and $\phirel$ is the relative phase between the radio frequency and the precession in the horizontal plane.
L000419	The corresponding spin dynamics is presented in detail in Refs.~\cite{Rathmann:2019lwi,PhysRevAccelBeams.27.111002}. Below, only the key elements are summarized.
L000420	
L000421	Solving Eq.~(\ref{eq:tbmt}) for this case, the fast vertical oscillation in Eq.~(\ref{eq:St}) is superimposed by a slow oscillation (also see Fig.~10 of Ref.~\cite{PhysRevAccelBeams.28.062801})
L000422	\begin{equation}\label{eq:oscwf}
L000423	S_y(t) = \cos(\phirel)\sin \left( 2\pi f_y t \right) .
L000424	\end{equation}
L000425	The frequency $f_y$ depends on the relative orientation of the magnetic-field axis $\vec m$ and the static invariant spin axis $\vec n$ in the absence of the rf excitation.
L000426	Aligned axes do not induce an oscillation, whereas a perpendicular configuration yields the maximal effect. If $\vec{m}$ is vertical, this sensitivity allows the rf Wien filter to probe the tilt of the invariant spin axis.
L000427	As experimental observable one can then define the resonance tune (or resonance strength) directly related to $\xiedm$:
L000428	\begin{equation}
L000429	    \epsilon = \frac{f_y}{\fc} = \frac{\psi_0}{4\pi}|\vec{n}\times\vec{m}| = \frac{\psi_0}{4\pi}|\sin(\xiedm)|.  \label{eq:resonance_strength}
L000430	\end{equation}
L000431	
L000432	This describes the ideal case where only the EDM contributes to the tilt of $\vec{n}$. In an actual ring, however, magnet misalignments, field imperfections, and orbit deviations introduce additional, position-dependent contributions $n_x^\mathrm{sys}$ and $n_z^\mathrm{sys}$
L000433	\begin{equation}
L000434	\vec n=\left(n_x^\mathrm{sys}-\xiedm,1,n_z^\mathrm{sys}\right) \;\mathrm{for}\; |\xiedm|,|n_x^\mathrm{sys}|,|n_z^\mathrm{sys}|\ll 1.
L000435	\end{equation}
L000436	Therefore, instead of aligning the rf Wien filter’s magnetic field axis $\vec{m}$ vertically and measuring only the build-up, we followed a different strategy.
L000437	The rf Wien filter could be rotated around the beam axis ($\phi_{\text{WF}}$) to introduce an additional $x$-component (roll) to $\vec{m}$, while a spin rotation in a Siberian snake located on the opposite side of the ring (see Fig.~\ref{fig:cosy}) added a $z$-component (pitch) to $\vec{n}$ ($\phi_\mathrm{snake}$).
L000438	For small additional tilts one gets
L000439	\begin{eqnarray}
L000440	\vec{n} &=& (n_x, 1, n_z + \phi_\mathrm{snake}), \\
L000441	\vec{m} &=& (\phi_\mathrm{WF}, 1, 0)
L000442	\end{eqnarray}
L000443	and using Eq.~(\ref{eq:resonance_strength}) $\epsilon^2$ can be written in leading order as
L000444	\begin{equation}
L000445	  \epsilon^2 = \frac{\psi_0^2}{16\pi^2}\left[\left(n_x-\phi_{\text{WF}}\right)^{2}+\left(n_z + \phi_\mathrm{snake}\right)^{2}\right]. \label{eq:res_strength_sib}
L000446	\end{equation}
L000447	
L000448	Eq.~(\ref{eq:res_strength_sib}) describes a two-dimensional paraboloid with the minimum corresponding to
L000449	$\vec{m}\parallel\vec{n}$, where the resonance strength vanishes. The corresponding angles $\phi_{\text{WF}}^{\text{min}}$ and $\phi_\mathrm{snake}^{\text{min}}$ are a measure of the direction of the invariant spin axis at the rf Wien filter location such that $\vec{n} = (\phi_{\text{WF}}^{\text{min}},1,-\phi_\mathrm{snake}^{\text{min}})$.
L000450	This approach facilitates a systematic study of how experimental uncertainties affect the extracted parameters and the final result.
