L000519	
L000520	The experimental method relies on the magnetic-field axis of the rf Wien filter as a reference for determining the orientation of the invariant spin axis.
L000521	While no direct experimental data exist on the magnetic-field direction within the rf Wien filter, extensive simulation studies have been performed, which explore the range of possible field errors perpendicular to the main axis~\cite{Slim:2016pim,Slim:2016dct,Slim:748558}.
L000522	Additional simulations were performed that explicitly account for the geometry of the installed rf Wien filter, following the approach described in Ref.~\cite{Slim:2016dct}.
L000523	The resulting possible spread corresponds to systematic uncertainties in the order of a few milliradians in the field orientation. The dominant contribution was found to originate from
L000524	a rotation of the beam axis in the horizontal plane. Further details are given in End Matter.
L000525	
L000526	Extensive beam and spin-tracking simulations to study the influence of known misalignments of the ring magnets and the beam-position monitors and the corresponding uncertainties
L000527	have been carried out.
L000528	No longitudinal or radial components of the invariant spin axis larger than \SI{1}{mrad} could be found~\cite{Vitz:998208}.
